ition of the target, which is a powerful and elegant path. The solution hinges on forming 23 from the remaining numbers: 19, 19, 37, 26, 40. The partial solution attempts to explore combinations like (40 + 19) - 37 = 22, and then tries to get to 23 via small adjustments — this shows a clear path toward constructing 23. Though it is cut off, the core idea is highly promising and mathematically grounded. This approach is more direct and elegant than brute-force trial, and if completed, would likely yield a valid solution.\n\ni2 and i3 both explore multiplication of large numbers and attempt to subtract combinations. While they consider plausible paths (e.g., 37×19=703), they fail to systematically connect the subtraction to the exact target or to form a complete expression. Their attempts are more exploratory and lack a clear, focused trajectory. They also do not leverage the factored form of 644 or any key insight like 28×23. These approaches are less efficient and more likely to fail without further refinement.\n\nThe most promising approaches are i0 and i1. i0 uses a multiplication-subtraction strategy with correct arithmetic, while i1 uses a deep insight into the factorization of 644 — a more elegant and likely successful path. Both are distinct in method and show strong potential. i0 is more concrete in its arithmetic, while i1 is more insightful in structure.\n\n<results>\ni0\ni1\n</results>', "i0 begins with a systematic exploration of additive combinations, checking sums of pairs and triplets to see how close they get to 126. While it stops mid-sentence and doesn't fully develop a path, the approach is structured and methodical—trying plausible additive combinations and analyzing deviations. This reflects a sound foundational strategy for solving such problems.\n\ni1 follows a nearly identical path to i0, reiterating the same additive checks and exploring subtraction possibilities. The trajectory is logically consistent and grounded, but it lacks innovation or