ectory to i2, especially in the decomposition of 792 - 13 = 779. While it has the same core idea, it is slightly less structured and cuts off mid-sentence without a clear path to building 9 or 13. It is redundant in spirit with i2.\n\nDespite surface similarities, i1 offers a distinct and creative alternative (using 79 * 9 = 711), which is a different multiplication base and a different residual target (68 instead of 13). This increases diversity in approach and shows a more varied exploration of possibilities.\n\ni2, while similar to i3, is more complete in its reasoning and includes a more structured attempt to build the required components. It is more likely to evolve into a correct solution due to its clear decomposition and better exploration of number relationships.\n\nTherefore, the two most diverse and promising approaches are:\n\n- i1: for its creative use of 79 * 9 = 711 and recognition of 69 as close to 68, offering a different multiplication base and a distinct path.\n- i2: for its structured decomposition (88 * 9 - 13 = 779) and more detailed attempt to generate 9 and 13 from available numbers, showing a higher potential to lead to a valid solution.\n\ni0 and i3 are less promising due to incomplete reasoning or redundancy.\n\n<results>\ni1\ni2\n</results>', 'i0 and i1 both correctly identify that 936 = 36 × 26 and focus on forming 26 from the remaining numbers. They explore valid arithmetic combinations (like 59 - 21 - 12 or 21 + 17 - 12) to achieve 26, which is mathematically sound. However, both fail to use all numbers — specifically, they ignore 59 and one of the 17s — which violates the requirement to use all given numbers. Despite this flaw, they are on the right path: recognizing the factorization and attempting to build the required subexpression. The difference between i0 and i1 lies in the specific expression used to form 26 (i0 uses 59 - 21 - 12, i1 uses 21 + 17 - 12), and both use only one 17. Still, both miss using the second 17 and 59, so n