h in i0 is not only more promising but also more diverse in its method—using a factored decomposition of the target and then constructing a sub-expression from available numbers—compared to the more brute-force and fragmented attempts in i1.\n\nNo other partial solutions are provided, so there is no comparison to other indices.\n\n<results>\ni0\n</results>', 'i0 begins by correctly computing the total sum of all numbers (444) and recognizing that 326 is less than that, suggesting a combination of addition and subtraction or multiplication. It then attempts a direct sum of four numbers (91 + 94 + 82 + 59 = 326), which works numerically but fails to use all six numbers as required by the problem statement. This is a critical flaw: it violates the explicit instruction to "use all of these numbers." While the arithmetic is sound, the solution is invalid due to missing usage of 53 and 65.\n\ni1 also identifies the total sum (444) and considers multiplication as a possibility, showing good analytical depth. It then finds a valid combination: 91 + 94 = 185 and 82 + 59 = 141, summing to 326. This is mathematically correct and reaches the target. However, like i0, it only uses four numbers (91, 94, 82, 59), neglecting 53 and 65. The flaw is identical — failure to use all numbers. The approach is promising in structure and insight, but it fails the core constraint.\n\nNeither i0 nor i1 uses all six numbers, which makes both invalid under the problem\'s requirement. However, i1 introduces a more sophisticated path by considering multiplication and exploring pairings to build toward the target, showing greater flexibility in problem decomposition. It demonstrates a more advanced reasoning process than i0, which is more brute-force and less exploratory.\n\nNo other partial solutions are provided, so we cannot compare against additional approaches. Among the given, i1 is the most diverse and promising because it:\n- Uses a structured pairing strategy,\n- Explores multiple combina